{"sdk_http_response":{"headers":{"x-gemini-service-tier":"standard","content-type":"application/json; charset=UTF-8","vary":"Origin, X-Origin, Referer","content-encoding":"gzip","date":"Tue, 17 Nov 2026 02:30:50 GMT","server":"scaffolding on HTTPServer2","x-xss-protection":"0","x-frame-options":"SAMEORIGIN","x-content-type-options":"nosniff","server-timing":"gfet4t7; dur=31931","alt-svc":"h3=\":443\"; ma=2592000,h3-29=\":443\"; ma=2592000","transfer-encoding":"chunked"},"body":null},"candidates":[{"content":{"parts":[{"media_resolution":null,"code_execution_result":null,"executable_code":null,"file_data":null,"function_call":null,"function_response":null,"inline_data":null,"text":"**Evaluation of Mathematical Claims**\n\nOkay, here's the breakdown of my evaluation of these twenty claims. I've gone through each one carefully, focusing on whether the subject, relation, object, and locator are accurately represented in the provided text. My primary concern is if the relation accurately reflects how the paper connects the subject and the object. Specifically, I'm checking if the subject \"proves\" the object when it should be something else.\n\nHere's my thought process for each claim:\n\n*   **Claim 1:** The claim has \"Z\\[S] for a profinite set S\" as the subject, \"proves\" as the relation, and a natural isomorphism as the object. The paper states an isomorphism within a proposition. The subject doesn't *prove* the isomorphism; the proposition does. Thus, the `wrong-relation`.\n*   **Claim 2:** The short exact sequence is said to \"prove\" exactness. However, the sequence *is* exact, it doesn't *prove* it. Again, a `wrong-relation` because the relation doesn't match the mathematical meaning.\n*   **Claim 3:** The claim states an \"equivalent-to\" relation between an expression and a power series algebra. The text uses an equality (\"=\"). This *is* faithful in the sense that \"=\" is an equivalence, but I will flag this as `faithful`.\n*   **Claim 4:** This claim is the same as claim 2. It's a sequence \"proving\" exactness. As before, this is a `wrong-relation`.\n*   **Claim 5:** The integral is said to \"define\" a specific representation. This matches the usage, so `faithful`.\n*   **Claim 6:** The remark \"depends-on\" a lemma. This is correct as stated, and I'll mark this as `faithful`.\n*   **Claim 7:** The proposition is said to \"prove\" the formation of an abelian subcategory. This is `faithful`.\n*   **Claim 8:** The category \"proves\" it is closed. This is a `wrong-relation`. The Theorem *states* that the category *has* a property.\n*   **Claim 9:** The subject \"Z\\[S]^square\" is defined as a limit. The text does state this equality, but the locator is incorrect, so this is `wrong-locator`. The definition is before Definition 0.2.4.\n*   **Claim 10:** The claim involves the definition of a \"condensed set\" as a sheaf. This aligns with the text, so this is `faithful`.\n*   **Claim 11:** The claim defines a condensed set as a functor. The text provides a more concrete description after the formal definition. The locator is incorrect, as this description appears *after* the initial definition, so this is `wrong-locator`.\n*   **Claim 12:** The claim states that \"condensed abelian groups\" \"prove\" the formation of an abelian category. This is a `wrong-relation`. The groups *form* the category; they don't *prove* it.\n*   **Claim 13:** The claim says Z\\[S] \"proves\" it is quasiseparated. The remark states this is the case, but it's not proved, so `wrong-relation`.\n*   **Claim 14:** The claim says Q \"defines\" the quotient of continuous maps. The text does introduce an expression for *Q*, so `faithful`.\n*   **Claim 15:** The theorem \"proves\" a module is solid if and only if ... This is a `faithful`.\n*   **Claim 16:** The functor \"proves\" it's fully faithful. The text says the functor *is* fully faithful. Thus, `wrong-relation`.\n*   **Claim 17:** The lemma \"proves\" a map is a homeomorphism. This is `faithful`.\n*   **Claim 18:** The claim is that a sequence \"depends-on\" an cohomology result. The text says that this result is *used* to *show* the formula for Q. So, it's either `faithful` or `wrong-relation`. I'll use `faithful` as an acceptable connection.\n*   **Claim 19:** The proposition \"proves\" a fully faithful functor. This is `faithful`.\n*   **Claim 20:** The theorem is said to \"prove\" the existence of an adjoint. There is no subsection (2) of Theorem 0.2.5. So, I will flag it with `wrong-locator`.\n\n\n","thought":true,"thought_signature":null,"video_metadata":null,"tool_call":null,"tool_response":null,"part_metadata":null,"audio_transcription":null,"media_processing":null},{"media_resolution":null,"code_execution_result":null,"executable_code":null,"file_data":null,"function_call":null,"function_response":null,"inline_data":null,"text":"{\"n\": 1, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"The paper does not state that the object Z[S] proves the isomorphism; rather, Proposition 0.2.1 proves it.\"}\n{\"n\": 2, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"A short exact sequence cannot 'prove' exactness; it has the property of exactness.\"}\n{\"n\": 3, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The text states that this quotient 'is the power series algebra' Z[[T]].\"}\n{\"n\": 4, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"The short exact sequence does not 'prove' its own exactness.\"}\n{\"n\": 5, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The paper explicitly defines the integral via the formula: 'one can form the integral \\\\int_S f \\\\mu := \\\\widetilde{f}(\\\\mu) \\\\in M'.\"}\n{\"n\": 6, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Remark 0.2.2 explicitly depends on the lemma: 'Moreover, by Lemma 0.1.3, the addition ... is continuous'.\"}\n{\"n\": 7, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Proposition 0.2.8 states exactly that profinite F_p-vector spaces form an abelian subcategory stable under limits, cokernels, and extensions.\"}\n{\"n\": 8, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"The category 'Solid' does not 'prove' these properties; it possesses them as stated in Theorem 0.2.5.\"}\n{\"n\": 9, \"grade\": \"wrong-locator\", \"reason\": \"The notation Z[S]^square is defined just before Definition 0.2.4, not within the definition itself.\"}\n{\"n\": 10, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Definition 0.1.1 directly defines a condensed set as a sheaf on the pro-étale site of the point.\"}\n{\"n\": 11, \"grade\": \"wrong-locator\", \"reason\": \"The concrete functorial definition is given in the unlabelled text after Definition 0.1.1, not inside it.\"}\n{\"n\": 12, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"Condensed abelian groups do not 'prove' they form an abelian category; they merely form one.\"}\n{\"n\": 13, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"Z[S] is an object and does not 'prove' that it is quasiseparated; Remark 0.2.2 states it.\"}\n{\"n\": 14, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The text introduces Q by specifying it satisfies 'Q(S) = Cont(S,R) / {locally constant maps}'.\"}\n{\"n\": 15, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Theorem 0.2.9 explicitly states exactly this equivalence for solid F_p-modules.\"}\n{\"n\": 16, \"grade\": \"wrong-relation\", \"reason\": \"The functor does not 'prove' it is fully faithful; it has the property of being fully faithful.\"}\n{\"n\": 17, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Lemma 0.1.3 explicitly proves this homeomorphism property for the natural map.\"}\n{\"n\": 18, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Footnote 5 states that the correctness of the formula for Q (and thus the exact sequence) is derived 'using H^1(S, M) = 0'.\"}\n{\"n\": 19, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Proposition 0.1.2(4) precisely proves this fully faithful functor from compactly generated weak Hausdorff spaces.\"}\n{\"n\": 20, \"grade\": \"wrong-locator\", \"reason\": \"Theorem 0.2.5 does not have numbered sub-items like '(2)', so the locator is incorrect.\"}","thoug